^\\circ = 1.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Simplify the Expression**:  \n   Substitute the identities \\( \\tan 45^\\circ = 1 \\) and \\( \\tan 44^\\circ \\cdot \\tan 46^\\circ = 1 \\):  \n   \\[\n   A = 15 \\times \\tan 44^\\circ \\times \\tan 45^\\circ \\times \\tan 46^\\circ = 15 \\times 1 \\times 1 = 15.\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \n\\(\\boxed{15}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "To solve for \\( A \\), we start by simplifying the expression given:\n\\[ \nA = 15 \\times \\tan 44^\\circ \\times \\tan 45^\\circ \\times \\tan 46^\\circ \n\\]\n\n1. **Step 1**: Recognize Values:\n    \\[\n    \\tan 45^\\circ = 1\n    \\]\n\n2. **Step 2**: Substitute:\n    \\[\n    A = 15 \\times \\tan 44^\\circ \\times 1 \\times \\tan 46^\\circ\n    \\]\n\n3. **Step 3**: Use Trigonometric Identity:\n    Recall the trigonometric identity involving tangents:\n    \\[\n    \\tan (\\theta) = \\cot (90^\\circ - \\theta)\n    \\]\n    Therefore,\n    \\[\n    \\tan 46^\\circ = \\cot (90^\\circ - 46^\\circ) = \\cot 44^\\circ\n    \\]\n    And we know,\n    \\[\n    \\cot \\theta = \\frac{1}{\\tan \\theta}\n    \\]\n    So,\n    \\[\n    \\tan 46^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\tan 44^\\circ}\n    \\]\n\n4. **Step 4**: Substitute and Simplify:\n    Substitute \\(\\tan 46^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\tan 44^\\circ}\\) into the expression for \\( A \\):\n    \\[\n    A = 15 \\times \\tan 44^\\circ \\times 1 \\times \\frac{1}{\\tan 44^\\circ}\n    \\]\n    Notice that \\(\\tan 44^\\circ \\times \\frac{1}{\\tan 44^\\circ} = 1\\), thus:\n    \\[\n    A = 15 \\times 1 = 15\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a candy store, a salesperson placed 91 candies of several kinds in a row on the counter. It turned out that between any two candies of the same kind, there is an even number of candies. What is the minimum number of kinds of candies that could be?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem where t