 VQ, D = AT x UV, thus points $ P, A, V, Q, U, T$ on conic curve $\\boxed{(H,K,D are collinear \\Leftrightarrow (1), thus (1) \\Leftrightarrow}$}.\n\nCombining all steps, $\\boxed{conclusions 1, 2}$ are equivalent $3 all equivalent.$\n\nHence proven. $\\bo\u0445ed$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let $\\alpha$, $\\beta$, and $\\gamma$ be the angles of triangle ABC. Prove that\n\na) $\\sin (\\alpha / 2) \\sin (\\beta / 2) \\sin (\\gamma / 2)=r / 4 R$\n\nb) $\\operatorname{tg}(\\alpha / 2) \\operatorname{tg}(\\beta / 2) \\operatorname{tg}(\\gamma / 2)=r / p$\n\nc) $\\cos (\\alpha / 2) \\cos (\\beta / 2) \\cos (\\gamma / 2)=p / 4 R$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove these three trigonometric identities related to the angles of a triangle ABC. The triangle has angles \u03b1, \u03b2, \u03b3, and the usual notation: r is the inradius, R is the circumradius, and p is the semiperimeter. Let me take them one by one. \n\nStarting with part (a): Show that sin(\u03b1/2) sin(\u03b2/2) sin(\u03b3/2) = r / (4R). \n\nFirst, I remember that in triangle geometry, there are various formulas connecting the trigonometric functions of the half-angles with the inradius and circumradius. Let me recall some of these. \n\nThe inradius r can be expressed in terms of the area and the semiperimeter: r = Area / p. Also, the area can be written using the circumradius: Area = (a b c) / (4R). So maybe combining these two gives r = (a b c) / (4R p). Hmm, not sure if that helps directly. \n\nAlternatively, there's a formula for the inradius in terms of the angles and the semiperimeter? Wait, perhaps using trigonometric identities. For example, I know that in a triangle, the inradius can be expressed as r = 4R sin(\u03b1/2) sin(\u03b2/2) sin(\u03b3/2). Wait, if that's the case, then rearranging gives the identity required for part (a). But wait, is that formula correct? Let me check. \n\nLet me recall another formula