nce I have expressions for sin and tan products, perhaps there's a relation between cos and these.\n\nAlternatively, using the half-angle formulas for cosine. The cosine of half an angle in a triangle is given by:\n\ncos(\u03b1/2) = \u221a[s (s - a) / (b c)]\n\nSimilarly for the other angles. Let me verify that. \n\nYes, the half-angle formula for cosine is:\n\ncos(\u03b1/2) = \u221a[(s (s - a)) / (b c)]\n\nSimilarly,\n\ncos(\u03b2/2) = \u221a[(s (s - b)) / (a c)]\n\ncos(\u03b3/2) = \u221a[(s (s - c)) / (a b)]\n\nTherefore, multiplying them together:\n\nProduct = \u221a[s (s - a) / (b c)] * \u221a[s (s - b) / (a c)] * \u221a[s (s - c) / (a b)]\n\nMultiply the numerators and denominators:\n\nNumerator inside sqrt: [s (s - a) * s (s - b) * s (s - c)] = s\u00b3 (s - a)(s - b)(s - c)\n\nDenominator inside sqrt: [b c * a c * a b] = a\u00b2 b\u00b2 c\u00b2\n\nTherefore, the product becomes sqrt[ s\u00b3 (s - a)(s - b)(s - c) / (a\u00b2 b\u00b2 c\u00b2) ) ]\n\nWhich is sqrt( s\u00b3 (s - a)(s - b)(s - c) ) / (a b c)\n\nAgain, from Heron's formula, we know that (s - a)(s - b)(s - c) = Area\u00b2 / s. Therefore,\n\nsqrt( s\u00b3 * (Area\u00b2 / s) ) / (a b c) = sqrt( s\u00b2 Area\u00b2 ) / (a b c) = (s Area) / (a b c)\n\nBut Area is also equal to (a b c)/(4 R), so substituting:\n\n(s * (a b c)/(4 R)) / (a b c) = s / (4 R)\n\nSince s is the semiperimeter, which here is denoted by p, this equals p / (4 R). Therefore, the product of the cosines is p / (4 R). Hence part (c) is proved.\n\nSo summarizing:\n\nFor part (a), using the half-angle formulas for sine, Heron's formula, and relationships between area, inradius, and circumradius.\n\nFor part (b), using the formula for tangent of half-angles in terms of inradius and semiperimeter minus sides, then relating to Heron's formula again.\n\nFor part (c), using half-angle formulas for cosine and Heron's formula, along with the expression for area in terms of circumradius.\n\nAll three identities are thus proven through these geometric relations and algebraic manipulations.\n\n**Final Answer**\n\na) \\boxed{\\dfrac{r}{4R}}\n\nb) \\boxed{\\dfrac{r}{p}}\n\nc) \\boxed{\\dfrac{p}{4R}}", "deepseek_solution": "To prove the given trigonometric identities for a triangle \\(ABC\\) with angles \\(\\alpha\\), \\(\\beta\\), \\(\\gamma\\), inradius \\(r\\), circumradius \\(R\\), and semiperimeter \\(p\\):\n\n### Part (a)\nWe need to show that \\(\\sin(\\alpha/2) \\sin(\\beta/2) \\sin(\\gamma/2) = r / (4R)\\).\n\n1. Using the half-angle formulas for sine in a triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(\\alpha/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{(s - b)(s - c)}{bc}}, \\quad \\sin(\\beta/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{(s - a)(s - c)}{ac}}, \\quad \\sin(\\gamma/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{(s - a)(s - b)}{ab}}\n   \\]\n2. Multiplying these together:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(\\alpha/2) \\sin(\\beta/2) \\sin(\\gamma/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)}{a^2b^2c^2}}\n   \\]\n3. Using Heron's formula, \\((s - a)(s - b)(s - c) = \\frac{\\text{Area}^2}{s}\\), and the area \\(\\text{Area} = \\frac{abc}{4R}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sin(\\alpha/2) \\sin(\\beta/2) \\sin(\\gamma/2) = \\frac{\\text{Area}^2 / s}{abc} = \\frac{(abc/(4R))^2 / s}{abc} = \\frac{r}{4R}\n   \\]\n\n### Part (b)\nWe need to show that \\(\\tan(\\alpha/2) \\tan(\\beta/2) \\tan(\\gamma/2) = r / p\\).\n\n1. Using the half-angle formulas for tangent in a triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\tan(\\alpha/2) = \\frac{r}{s - a}, \\quad \\tan(\\beta/2) = \\frac{r}{s - b}, \\quad \\tan(\\gamma/2) = \\frac{r}{s - c}\n   \\]\n2. Multiplying these together:\n   \\[\n   \\tan(\\alpha/2) \\tan(\\beta/2) \\tan(\\gamma/2) = \\frac{r^3}{(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)}\n   \\]\n3. Using Heron's formula and the relationship \\((s - a)(s - b)(s - c) = r^2s\\):\n   \\[\n   \\tan(\\alpha/2) \\tan(\\beta/2) \\tan(\\gamma/2) = \\frac{r^3}{r^2s} = \\frac{r}{s} = \\frac{r}{p}\n   \\]\n\n### Part (c)\nWe need to show that \\(\\cos(\\alpha/2) \\cos(\\beta/2) \\cos(\\gamma/2) = p / (4R)\\).\n\n1. Using the half-angle formulas for cosine in a triangle:\n   \\[\n   \\cos(\\alpha/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{s(s - a)}{bc}}, \\quad \\cos(\\beta/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{s(s - b)}{ac}}, \\quad \\cos(\\gamma/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{s(s - c)}{ab}}\n   \\]\n2. Multiplying these together:\n   \\[\n   \\cos(\\alpha/2) \\cos(\\beta/2) \\cos(\\gamma/2) = \\sqrt{\\frac{s^3(s - a)(s - b)(s - c)}{a^2b^2c^2}}\n   \\]\n3. Using Heron's formula and the area \\(\\text{Area} = \\frac{abc}{4R}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\cos(\\alpha/2) \\cos(\\beta/2) \\cos(\\gamma/2) = \\frac{s \\cdot \\text{Area}}{abc} = \\frac{s \\cdot (abc/(4R))}{abc} = \\frac{s}{4R} = \\frac{p}{4R}\n   \\]\n\n### Final Answers\n\na) \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{r}{4R}}\\)\n\nb) \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{r}{p}}\\)\n\nc) \\(\\boxed{\\dfrac{p}{4R}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n\n**Given problem:** \nLet $\\alpha, \\beta$, and $\\gamma$ be the angles of triangle $ABC$. Prove the following:\n1. \\[\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{4R}\\]\n2. \\[\\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{p}\\]\n3. \\[\\cos \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{p}{4R}\\]\n\n**Solution:**\n\n### Part (a): \n\\[\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{4R}\\]\n\n1. Let's denote $O$ as the center of the inscribed circle and $K$ as the point of tangency of the inscribed circle with side $AB$. The tangents from a point to a circle are equal; thus:\n   \\[AK = r \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)\\]\n   \\[BK = r \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)\\]\n2. The length of side $AB$ can be written using Law of Sines:\n   \\[2R \\sin \\gamma = AB\\]\n3. The side $AB$ is also sum of segments created by tangents from $A$ and $B$:\n   \\[AB = AK + KB = r \\left(\\cot \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) + \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\right)\\]\n4. Using the identity for cotangent sum:\n   \\[\\cot \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) + \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) = \\frac{\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{2}\\right)}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}\\]\n5. Hence,\n   \\[AB = r \\frac{\\cos \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}\\]\n6. Equating these expressions for $AB$:\n   \\[2R \\sin \\gamma = r \\frac{\\cos \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}\\]\n7. Using the identity \\(\\sin \\gamma = 2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\\), we have:\n   \\[2R \\times 2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = r \\frac{\\cos \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}\\]\n8. Simplifying gives:\n   \\[4R \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = r \\frac{1}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}\\]\n9. Dividing both sides by \\( \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) \\):\n   \\[4R \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = r \\frac{1}{\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)}\\]\n\n10.  Finally:\n    \\[\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{4R}\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\\boxed{\\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{4R}}\\]\n\n### Part (b):\n\\[\\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{p}\\]\n\n1. Given the relations\n   \\[ p-a = r \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)\\]\n   \\[ p-b = r \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right)\\]\n   \\[ p-c = r \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\\]\n\n2. Multiplying these equalities:\n   \\[\n   (p-a) \\times (p-b) \\times (p-c) = r^3 \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n3. Applying the fact:\n   \\[\n   p(p-a)(p-b)(p-c) = r^2 (\\text{area of } ABC)^2 = (pr)^2\n   \\]\n4. Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   r^2 = r^3 \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n5. Dividing both sides by \\( p \\cdot r \\):\n   \\[\n   \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cot \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{p}\n   \\]\n6. Finally:\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{p}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\\boxed{\\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{p}}\\]\n\n### Part (c):\n\\[\\cos \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\cos \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{p}{4R}\\]\n\n1. Using the previously proven parts:\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\sin \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{4R} \\quad \\text{(Part a)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\alpha}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\beta}{2}\\right) \\operatorname{tg} \\left(\\frac{\\gamma}{2}\\right) = \\frac{r}{p} \\quad \\text{(Part b)}\n   \\]\n\n2. Recall that \n   \\[\n   \\operatorn