, we had BD\u00b2 = \u03b1\u00b2 + 1 - 2\u03b1 cos \u03b1. Similarly, the other diagonal AC can be found using the formula for the other diagonal in a parallelogram: AC\u00b2 = AB\u00b2 + AD\u00b2 + 2 AB * AD * cos(angle BAD). Since in a parallelogram, the sum of the squares of the diagonals equals twice the sum of the squares of the sides. Wait, actually, the formula is:\n\nAC\u00b2 + BD\u00b2 = 2(AB\u00b2 + AD\u00b2)\n\nSo since BD\u00b2 = \u03b1\u00b2 + 1 - 2\u03b1 cos \u03b1,\n\nthen AC\u00b2 = 2(\u03b1\u00b2 + 1) - BD\u00b2 = 2\u03b1\u00b2 + 2 - (\u03b1\u00b2 + 1 - 2\u03b1 cos \u03b1) = \u03b1\u00b2 + 1 + 2\u03b1 cos \u03b1.\n\nTherefore, AC = sqrt(\u03b1\u00b2 + 1 + 2\u03b1 cos \u03b1).\n\nSo the lengths of the diagonals are BD = sqrt(\u03b1\u00b2 + 1 - 2\u03b1 cos \u03b1) and AC = sqrt(\u03b1\u00b2 + 1 + 2\u03b1 cos \u03b1).\n\nFor the circles around opposite vertices to overlap, the distance between the centers (the diagonal) should be \u2264 2*radius = 2*1 = 2. So if BD \u2264 2 and AC \u2264 2, then circles around opposite vertices would overlap. But maybe even if the diagonals are longer than 2, the circles from adjacent vertices could cover the parallelogram.\n\nBut since the problem states that the four circles cover the parallelogram if and only if \u03b1 \u2264 cos \u03b1 + \u221a3 sin \u03b1, perhaps the critical point is when the furthest point from all vertices is exactly at distance 1 from some vertex.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the farthest points in the parallelogram are the midpoints of the sides. Let's check.\n\nTake side AB: length \u03b1. The midpoint of AB is at distance 0.5\u03b1 from A and B. Since the circles have radius 1, as long as 0.5\u03b1 \u2264 1, which would mean \u03b1 \u2264 2. But if \u03b1 > 2, the midpoint of AB would be further than 1 from both A and B. But in that case, since AD is 1, maybe the circle at D or C covers that area? Wait, but if AB is longer than 2, the midpoint is more than 1 away from A and B, but how far is it