s check if \\( f(x) \\) has symmetry with respect to the line \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\).\n\n3. We calculate \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x\\right) \\):\n   \n   \\[\n   f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x\\right) \\sin 2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x\\right).\n   \\]\n   \n   Using trigonometric identities \\( \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x\\right) = \\sin x \\) and \\( 2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x\\right) = \\pi - 2x \\):\n   \n   \\[\n   = \\sin x \\sin(\\pi - 2x)\n   \\]\n   \n   Since \\( \\sin(\\pi - 2x) = \\sin 2x \\):\n   \n   \\[\n   = \\sin x \\cdot \\sin 2x \n   \\]\n   \n4. Now, calculate \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + x\\right) \\):\n   \n   \\[\n   f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + x\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + x\\right) \\sin 2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + x\\right).\n   \\]\n   \n   Using trigonometric identities \\( \\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + x\\right) = -\\sin x \\) and \\( 2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + x\\right) = \\pi + 2x \\):\n   \n   \\[\n   = -\\sin x \\cdot \\sin(\\pi + 2x)\n   \\]\n   \n   Since \\( \\sin(\\pi + 2x) = -\\sin 2x \\):\n   \n   \\[\n   = (-\\sin x)(-\\sin 2x) = \\sin x \\cdot \\sin 2x \n   \\]\n   \nSince \\( f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - x\\right) = f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} + x\\right) \\), \\( f(x) \\) is symmetric about the line \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\). Therefore, statement B is correct.\n\n#### Statement C: \"The equation \\( f(x) = \\frac{7}{9} \\) has a solution.\"\n\nConsider the absolute value of \\( f(x) \\):\n   \n5. Simplify using trigonometric identities:\n   \n   \\[\n   f(x) = \\cos x \\sin 2x = \\cos x (2 \\sin x \\cos x) = 2 \\sin x \\cos^2 x\n   \\]\n   \n   Therefore:\n   \n   \\[\n   |f(x)| = |2 \\sin x \\cos^2 x| = 2 |\\sin x| |\\cos^2 x|\n   \\]\n   \nGiven that \\( |\\sin x| \\leq 1 \\) and \\( |\\cos x| \\leq 1 \\):\n   \n6. Consider the maximum value:\n   \n   \\[\n   |f(x)| \\leq 2 |1| |1|^2 = 2\n   \\]\n\n7. The maximum value of \\( |f(x)| \\):\n   \n   \\[ \n   \\leq \\sqr