lambda)}{f(\\lambda) \\cos A \\cdot \\cos C} \\) is constant. Using trigonometric identities and the result from part (1), we express \\( \\cos A \\) and \\( \\cos C \\) in terms of \\( \\tan \\frac{A}{2} \\) and \\( \\tan \\frac{C}{2} \\). Through detailed analysis and verification with specific examples, we find that the function \\( f(\\lambda) = -\\frac{2\\lambda}{\\lambda^2 + 1} \\) makes the expression constant (specifically \\(-1\\)) for all valid triangles.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{ f(\\lambda) = -\\dfrac{2\\lambda}{\\lambda^2 + 1} }\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n\nLet's denote the angles in the triangle $ABC$ such that $\\angle A = A$, $\\angle B = B$, and $\\angle C = C$, with side lengths opposite to these angles denoted as $a$, $b$, and $c$, respectively. Given: $a + c = \\lambda b$, where $\\lambda > 1$.\n\n**Part (1): Proof of $\\tan \\left( \\frac{A}{2} \\right) \\cdot \\tan \\left( \\frac{C}{2} \\right) = \\frac{\\lambda - 1}{\\lambda + 1}$**\n\n1. Using the Law of Sines, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a}{\\sin A} = \\frac{b}{\\sin B} = \\frac{c}{\\sin C}\n   \\]\n   From the given $a + c = \\lambda b$, substituting using the Law of Sines:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a}{\\sin A} + \\frac{c}{\\sin C} = \\lambda \\frac{b}{\\sin B}\n   \\]\n   \n2. Express $\\sin A$ and $\\sin C$ using their complementary angle identities:\n   \\[\n   \\sin A + \\sin C = \\lambda \\sin B\n   \\]\n   Utilizing the Sine Addition Formula:\n   \\[\n   2 \\sin \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right) = \\lambda \\cdot 2 \\sin \\left( \\frac{B}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{B}{2} \\right)\n   \\]\n   \n3. Simplify:\n   \\[\n   \\sin \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right) = \\lambda \\sin \\left( \\frac{B}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{B}{2} \\right)\n   \\]\n   \n4. Since $A + B + C = \\pi$, it follows that $\\frac{A + C}{2} + \\frac{B}{2} = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$:\n   \\[\n   \\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right) = \\lambda \\cos \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right)\n   \\]\n   \n5. Write the tangent half-angle identity:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right) - \\cos \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right)}{\\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right) + \\cos \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right)} = \\frac{\\lambda - 1}{\\lambda + 1}\n   \\]\n   \n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\tan \\left( \\frac{A}{2} \\right) \\cdot \\tan \\left( \\frac{C}{2} \\right) = \\frac{\\lambda - 1}{\\lambda + 1}\n   \\]\n   \n**Part (2): Existence of function $f(\\lambda)$**\n\n1. From Part (1):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\left( \\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right) - \\cos \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right) \\right)^2 + \\left( \\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right) + \\cos \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right) \\right)^2}{\\left( \\cos \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right) + \\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right) \\right)^2 - \\left( \\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right) - \\cos \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right) \\right)^2}\n   \\]\n\n2. Simplify and evaluate:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(\\lambda - 1)^2 + (\\lambda + 1)^2}{(\\lambda + 1)^2 - (\\lambda - 1)^2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{\\lambda^2 + 1}{2\\lambda}\n   \\]\n   \n3. Using trigonometric identities:\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{\\cos^2 \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right) + \\cos^2 \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right)}{2 \\cos \\left( \\frac{A+C}{2} \\right) \\cos \\left( \\frac{A-C}{2} \\right)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1 + \\frac{1}{2}(\\cos (A+C) + \\cos (A-C))}{\\cos A + \\cos C}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1 + \\cos A \\cos C}{\\cos A + \\cos C}\n   \\]\n\n4. Therefore, taking $f(\\lambda) = \\frac{-2\\lambda}{\\lambda^2 + 1}$:\n   \\[\n   f(\\lambda) (1 + \\cos A \\cos C) + \\cos A + \\cos C = 0\n   \\]\n\n5. Thus:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{\\cos A + \\cos C + f(\\lambda)}{f(\\lambda) \\cos A \\cos C} = -1\n   \\]\n\nThe function $f(\\lambda) = -\\frac{2\\lambda}{\\lambda^2 + 1}$ satisfies the condition, so it exists.\n\nConclusion: \\( \\boxed{-1} \\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cas