 \nAssume \\( x_k \\geq 0 \\) for all \\( k \\leq n \\). We need to show \\( x_{n+1} \\geq 0 \\). Consider two cases:\n\n1. **Case 1: \\( a \\leq 0 \\)**  \n   Here, \\( a e^{x_n} \\leq 0 \\) because \\( a \\leq 0 \\) and \\( e^{x_n} > 0 \\). Thus,  \n   \\[\n   x_{n+1} = 1 - a e^{x_n} \\geq 1 - 0 = 1 \\geq 0.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Case 2: \\( 0 < a \\leq 1 \\)**  \n   We first show \\( x_{n} \\leq \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\) for all \\( n \\geq 0 \\):  \n   - **Base Case:** \\( x_0 = 0 \\leq \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\) since \\( \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\geq 0 \\).  \n   - **Inductive Step:** Assume \\( x_k \\leq \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\) for all \\( k \\leq n \\). Then,  \n     \\[\n     x_{n+1} = 1 - a e^{x_n} \\leq 1 - a e^{0} = 1 - a.\n     \\]\n     Using the inequality \\( 1 - a \\leq \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\) (valid for \\( 0 < a \\leq 1 \\)), we get  \n     \\[\n     x_{n+1} \\leq \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right).\n     \\]\n   Hence, \\( x_n \\leq \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\) for all \\( n \\).  \n\n   Since \\( x_n \\leq \\ln\\left(\\frac{1}{a}\\right) \\), we have \\( e^{x_n} \\leq \\frac{1}{a} \\). Therefore,  \n   \\[\n   a e^{x_n} \\leq 1 \\implies x_{n+1} = 1 - a e^{x_n} \\geq 0.\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nBy induction, \\( x_n \\geq 0 \\) holds for all positive integers \\( n \\) when \\( a \\leq 1 \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_n \\geq 0}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Case when $a \\leq 0$:**\n\n   - Given the recursive equation: \\( x_{n+1} = 1 - a e^{x_n} \\).\n   - If \\( a \\leq 0 \\), then \\( -a \\geq 0 \\), meaning $a$ is non-positive.\n   - Start with the initial condition: \\( x_0 = 0 \\).\n\n   For \\( n = 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   x_1 = 1 - a e^{x_0} = 1 - a e^{0} = 1 - a.\n   \\]\n   Since \\( a \\leq 0 \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   x_1 = 1 - a \\geq 1.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\( n \\geq 1 \\):\n   Suppose by induction \\( x_n \\geq 1 \\). Then for \\( n+1 \\):\n   \\[\n   x_{n+1} = 1 - a e^{x_n} \\geq 1 - a e^{1}.\n   \\]\n   Since \\( a \\leq 0 \\) and \\( e^{x_n} \\geq 1 \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   1 - a e^{x_n} \\geq 1 \\implies x_{n+1} \\geq 1.\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore, for any \\( n \\geq 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   x_n \\geq 1.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Case when \\( 0 < a \\leq 1 \\):**\n\n   - Given the function \\( f(x) = 1 - a e^{x} \\).\n   - We note that the initial condition is \\( x_0 = 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   x_1 = f(0) = 1 - a e^{0} = 1 - a.\n   \\]\n   - The function \\( f(x) = 1 - a e^{x} \\) is strictly decreasing because:\n     \\[\n     f'(x) = -a e^{x} < 0 \\quad \\text{(for all \\( x \\))}.\n     \\]\n\n   Suppose \\( x_n \\in [0, 1-a] \\). Then we need to show \\( x_{n+1} \\in [0, 1-a] \\):\n   \\[\n   x_{n+1} = f(x_n) \\in [f(1-a), f(0)].\n   \\]\n\n   Calculate \\( f(0) \\):\n   \\[\n   f(0) = 1 - a.\n   \\]\n   \n   Calculate \\( f(1-a) \\):\n   \\[\n   f(1-a) = 1 - a e^{1-a}.\n   \\]\n   We need to show \\( 1 - a e^{1-a} \\geq 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   1 - \\frac{a}{e^{a-1}} \\geq 1 - \\frac{a}{1 + (a-1)} = 0.\n   \\]\n   By ensuring \\( a \\leq 1 \\), we guarantee:\n   \\[\n   1 - a e^{1-a} \\geq 0.\n   \\]\n\n   So:\n   \\[\n   f(x_n) \\in [0, 1-a].\n   \\]\n\n   Thus, by induction \\( x_{n+1} \\in [0, 1-a] \\).\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nFor any real number \\( a \\leq 1 \\), and any non-negative integer \\( n \\), we always have:\n\\[ \nx_n \\geq 0.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{x_n \\geq 0}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The students in class 5A had a total of 2015 pencils. One of them lost a box containing five pencils and replaced it with a box containing 50 pencils. How many pencils do the students in class 5A have now?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so the problem says that the students in class 5A originally had a total of 2015 pencils. Then, one of them lost a box with five pencils and replaced it with a box containing 50 pencils. We need to figure out how many pencils they have now.\n\nLet me start 