rt{3} + s_n \\sqrt{6} \\)\n\nWait, actually, I need to sum all the contributions:\n\nWait, hold on. Wait, when you multiply out, you have three separate multiplications (from 1, sqrt2, sqrt3) each multiplied by the original expression. So the total is:\n\nConstant terms: only from 1*p_n and sqrt2*q_n*2 and sqrt3*r_n*3 ?\n\nWait no. Wait, let me do this step by step.\n\nFirst, the original multiplication:\n\nLeft-hand side is \\( (1 + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3}) \\times (p_n + q_n \\sqrt{2} + r_n \\sqrt{3} + s_n \\sqrt{6}) \\)\n\nBreaking it down:\n\n1*(p_n) = p_n\n\n1*(q_n \\sqrt{2}) = q_n \\sqrt{2}\n\n1*(r_n \\sqrt{3}) = r_n \\sqrt{3}\n\n1*(s_n \\sqrt{6}) = s_n \\sqrt{6}\n\nsqrt2*(p_n) = p_n sqrt2\n\nsqrt2*(q_n sqrt2) = q_n * 2\n\nsqrt2*(r_n sqrt3) = r_n sqrt6\n\nsqrt2*(s_n sqrt6) = s_n * sqrt12 = s_n * 2 sqrt3\n\nsqrt3*(p_n) = p_n sqrt3\n\nsqrt3*(q_n sqrt2) = q_n sqrt6\n\nsqrt3*(r_n sqrt3) = r_n * 3\n\nsqrt3*(s_n sqrt6) = s_n * sqrt18 = s_n * 3 sqrt2\n\nNow, collect all terms by type:\n\nConstant terms (no radicals):\n\n- From sqrt2*(q_n sqrt2): 2 q_n\n\n- From sqrt3*(r_n sqrt3): 3 r_n\n\nTotal constant term: 2 q_n + 3 r_n\n\nTerms with sqrt2:\n\n- From 1*(q_n sqrt2): q_n sqrt2\n\n- From sqrt2*(p_n): p_n sqrt2\n\n- From sqrt3*(s_n sqrt6): 3 s_n sqrt2\n\nTotal sqrt2 terms: (q_n + p_n + 3 s_n) sqrt2\n\nTerms with sqrt3:\n\n- From 1*(r_n sqrt3): r_n sqrt3\n\n- From sqrt2*(s_n sqrt6): 2 s_n sqrt3\n\n- From sqrt3*(p_n): p_n sqrt3\n\nTotal sqrt3 terms: (r_n + 2 s_n + p_n) sqrt3\n\nTerms with sqrt6:\n\n- From 1*(s_n sqrt6): s_n sqrt6\n\n- From sqrt2*(r_n sqrt3): r_n sqrt6\n\n- From sqrt3*(q_n sqrt2): q_n sqrt6\n\nTotal sqrt6 terms: (s_n + r_n + q_n) sqrt6\n\nTherefore, combining all these, the product is:\n\n[2 q_n + 3 r_n] + [q_n + p_n + 3 s_n] sqrt2 + [r_n + 2 s_n + p_n] sqrt3 + [s_n + r_n + q_n] sqrt6\n\nBut this product is equal to \\( (1 + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3})^{n+1} = p_{n+1} + q_{n+1} \\sqrt{2} + r_{n+1} \\sqrt{3} + s_{n+1} \\sqrt{6} \\).\n\nTherefore, by equating coefficients, we get the recurrence relations:\n\n- \\( p_{n+1} = 2 q_n + 3 r_n \\)\n\n- \\( q_{n+1} = q_n + p_n + 3 s_n \\)\n\n- \\( r_{n+1} = r_n + 2 s_n + p_n \\)\n\n- \\( s_{n+1} = s_n + r_n + q_n \\)\n\nSo these are four recurrence relations linking the coefficients at step \\( n+1 \\) to those at step \\( n \\). This is a system of linear recurrences which can be represented in matrix form. \n\nLet me write the system as:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{pmatrix}\np_{n+1} \\\\\nq_{n+1} \\\\\nr_{n+1} \\\\\ns_{n+1}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\\n1 & 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\\n1 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\\n0 & 1 & 1 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\np_n \\\\\nq_n \\\\\nr_n \\\\\ns_n\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nYes, this matrix captures the coefficients from the recurrence relations. Let me check:\n\n- For \\( p_{n+1} \\), coefficients are 0*p_n + 2*q_n + 3*r_n + 0*s_n.\n\n- For \\( q_{n+1} \\), coefficients are 1*p_n + 1*q_n + 0*r_n + 3*s_n.\n\n- For \\( r_{n+1} \\), coefficients are 1*p_n + 0*q_n + 1*r_n + 2*s_n.\n\n- For \\( s_{n+1} \\), coefficients are 0*p_n + 1*q_n + 1*r_n + 1*s_n.\n\nYes, that's correct.\n\nTherefore, if we denote the vector \\( \\mathbf{v}_n = \\begin{pmatrix} p_n \\\\ q_n \\\\ r_n \\\\ s_n \\end{pmatrix} \\), then \\( \\mathbf{v}_{n+1} = M \\mathbf{v}_n \\), where \\( M \\) is the above matrix. Therefore, \\( \\mathbf{v}_n = M^n \\mathbf{v}_0 \\). The initial vector \\( \\mathbf{v}_0 \\) corresponds to \\( n = 0 \\), so \\( (1 + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3})^0 = 1 \\), which is \\( 1 + 0\\sqrt{2} + 0\\sqrt{3} + 0\\sqrt{6} \\). Therefore, \\( \\mathbf{v}_0 = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\).\n\nTo find the behavior as \\( n \\to \\infty \\), we need to analyze the dominant eigenvalue of the matrix \\( M \\). The reason is that as \\( n \\) increases, the vector \\( \\mathbf{v}_n \\) will align with the dominant eigenvector of \\( M \\), and the ratios between the components will approach the ratios in the dominant eigenvector. Therefore, the limits \\( \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} p_n/q_n \\), etc., should be the ratios of the corresponding components in the dominant eigenvector.\n\nTherefore, the next step is to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the matrix \\( M \\). The dominant eigenvalue (the one with the largest magnitude) will dictate the exponential growth rate, and the corresponding eigenvector will give the ratios between \\( p_n, q_n, r_n, s_n \\).\n\nCalculating eigenvalues of a 4x4 matrix can be tedious, but maybe we can relate it to the original number \\( \\alpha = 1 + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3} \\). Notice that \\( \\alpha \\) is the original number whose powers we are expanding. Since \\( \\alpha \\) is an eigenvalue of the matrix \\( M \\), because when we raise \\( \\alpha \\) to the power \\( n \\), the coefficients \\( p_n, q_n, r_n, s_n \\) are related by the matrix \\( M \\).\n\nAlternatively, perhaps \\( \\alpha \\) is an eigenvalue of the matrix \\( M \\). Let me check.\n\nSuppose \\( \\lambda \\) is an eigenvalue of \\( M \\), then we have \\( M \\mathbf{v} = \\lambda \\mathbf{v} \\) for some non-zero vector \\( \\mathbf{v} \\). Let's assume that \\( \\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} p \\\\ q \\\\ r \\\\ s \\end{pmatrix} \\). Then:\n\nFrom the first equation: \\( 0*p + 2*q + 3*r + 0*s = \\lambda p \\)\n\nSecond equation: 1*p + 1*q + 0*r + 3*s = \\lambda q\n\nThird equation: 1*p + 0*q + 1*r + 2*s = \\lambda r\n\nFourth equation: 0*p + 1*q + 1*r + 1*s = \\lambda s\n\nThis system of equations should hold for non-trivial \\( p, q, r, s \\). If \\( \\lambda = \\alpha \\), then perhaps this system is satisfied?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the eigenvalues of \\( M \\) are related to \\( \\alpha \\) and its conjugates. Since the minimal polynomial of \\( \\alpha \\) is quartic, as we found earlier, the eigenvalues might be \\( \\alpha \\) and its conjugates. \n\nAlternatively, note that \\( (1 + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3})^n = p_n + q_n \\sqrt{2} + r_n \\sqrt{3} + s_n \\sqrt{6} \\), so if we l